Context: this page continues the proof of Corollary 2.6 from the previous page. Let X be smooth projective connected of dimension n ≥ 2, let ξ_1,…,ξ_{n-1} be ample classes in Pic(X), and set η = ξ_1⋯ξ_{n-1}. The corollary claims two things. First, cup product with η, H^1(X,Q_ℓ) → H^{2n-1}(X,Q_ℓ)(n-1), is an isomorphism. Second, its inverse is induced by a canonical Q-divisorial correspondence, independent of ℓ. This is the hard Lefschetz theorem in degree 1, together with the Lefschetz standard conjecture B(X) in degree 1.

Translation into abelian varieties. Write B_X for the Picard variety (Pic^0_red) and A_X for the Albanese variety, so that A_X ≅ B_X^* (duality). H^1 corresponds to the Tate module of B_X and H^{2n-1}(n-1) to that of A_X. The class η then gives a homomorphism u: B_X → A_X. The page says it suffices to show u is an isogeny (finite kernel). Better, one can show u is a polarization of B_X. The inverse correspondence is then u^{-1} ∈ Hom(A_X,B_X)⊗Q, a Q-divisor class on A_X×B_X, pulled back to X×X.

First attempt (crossed out). Factor u through iterated ample hyperplane sections X = X_0 ⊃ X_1 ⊃ … ⊃ X_{n-2} = Y. Here Y is a smooth surface, cut out by n-2 general very ample divisors L_1,…,L_{n-2}. The factorization is B_X →ρ B_Y →λ A_Y →σ A_X, where:
- ρ is restriction;
- λ is the polarization-type map defined by the last class L_{n-1}|Y on the surface Y;
- σ is the push-forward induced by the inclusion Y ⊂ X.
By weak Lefschetz, ρ and σ are isogenies, and they are transposes (duals) of each other. So u = ρ^t ∘ λ ∘ ρ, reducing the problem to a surface.

Second approach (kept). Use the obvious functoriality, in X and Y, of the homomorphism B_X → A_Y attached in §2.1 to a correspondence Z. Suppose Z is the image of a morphism v: C → X×Y from a smooth projective curve, with components v_1: C → X and v_2: C → Y. Then the homomorphism factors as B_X →v_1^* B_C = J_C ≅ A_C (canonical principal polarization of the Jacobian) →v_{2*} A_Y. In other words, the associated divisorial class on B_X×B_Y is the pull-back of the canonical theta class on B_C×B_C.

Taking X = Y, v_1 = v_2 = v (next page), the following become equivalent:
- (i) the map B_X → A_X is a polarization;
- (ii) it is an isogeny;
- (iii) v^*: B_X → J_C has finite kernel;
- (iv) v_*: J_C → A_X is surjective.
Applied to C a complete-intersection curve of the ample divisors, (iii)/(iv) hold by Lefschetz/Weil. Hence u is the pull-back of the theta polarization of J_C, and so a polarization. This is the classical argument, going back to Weil and Matsusaka, that the Picard variety receives a polarization from a hyperplane-section curve.
